<h1>LiveView at the edge</h1>
<p class="lead">This counter is a Soli <strong>LiveView</strong>: the page opens a WebSocket,
every click goes to a handler written in Soli, and the server sends back a patch of the
markup. Here the socket ends in a <strong>Cloudflare Durable Object</strong>. Every visitor
shares this board: open the page in two windows and click.</p>

<div data-live-root data-live-room="edge-demo" data-liveview-url="/live/socket/tally">
  <p class="muted">Connecting…</p>
</div>

<h2>How it runs</h2>
<ul>
  <li>The Worker sends <code>/live/socket/tally</code> to one Durable Object per LiveView
      instance; this board is a room (<code>live_rooms("tally")</code>), so it has one for
      everybody.</li>
  <li>The object accepts the socket with the <strong>hibernation API</strong> and keeps the
      LiveView's state and last markup in its storage. Between clicks it can be evicted; the
      next click wakes it, reads the state back and runs one frame.</li>
  <li>A frame is the same code as under <code>soli serve</code>: the handler in
      <code>app/controllers/live_controller.sl</code>, the template in
      <code>app/views/live/tally.html.slv</code>, the same diff and the same client
      (<code>/live/client.js</code>).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Not on the edge yet</h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>tick</code> intervals, live queries (<code>Model.live_where</code>) and
      <code>live_update</code> from a controller or a job: they need a process that stays up.</li>
  <li>Uploads through <code>soli-upload</code>.</li>
</ul>

<script src="/live/client.js?v=2"></script>
